Children and TV

TV has a central place in the lives of children today, which is both good and  bad. Every 3-year-old knows how to turn the TV on and how to control it with the remote. As a result, parents have a huge responsibility to use the medium in the right way, which is mainly a question of controlling quantity and quality. The TV companies provide too many programs, which gives us an opportunity to choose what to watch. But choosing is difficult for children because they want to see everything. It's up to their parents to decide for them what they should watch.
First, the time that the children spend in front of the TV should be limited. Some children spend multiple hours a day watching TV. Children need to be physically active and staring at a screen for a long time can't be good for your eyes. But there are also psychological risks. Children who spend too much time in front of the television can become passive, they can lose their imagination and the ability find something to do on their own.
It is also important to help children choose what to watch. There are a lot of good children's programs to choose from, but there are also a lot of rubbish. Too many supposedly for-children programs contain a lot of violence. Consider the main plot in the series 'Pokémon', which is about cruel competitions between fantasy animals in a 'Pokémon league', and this series is far from the worst one.
If the parents manage to control the quantity and to some extent the quality of the programs, and provide their children with books to read from an early age, I think children can actually benefit from watching TV. While TV can be addictive and cause passive behavior at its worst, TV at its best can be educational and inspiring. Many children in Sweden are very well informed about various subjects, all thanks to watching TV. They learn about current events by watching news adapted for children, which are explained in such a way that children can understand. They learn about nature and science, and not only from 'Hjärnkontoret', a Swedish science program for children, but also from programs aimed at adults.
Watching TV can also inspire children to new activities. For example, you will occasionally find children playing 'Gladiators' in the playgrounds, and almost every school has a 'Ministar' competition with children imitating pop stars. And watching sports is always inspiring for children! It is a well-known fact that when a national athlete does well in international competitions, a lot of children want to start practicing that branch of sport. Since television has made it possible to see the competitions, the phenomenon has increased, even though it has for a long time been the case.
It should be added that even programs of better quality, such as TV versions of good books, might cause passive behavior. Watching TV is easy, and you are given pictures to help you follow what's going on. You never have to create your own image of what a character looks like, and you are never given time to reflect on their thoughts or feelings. After getting used to this easy way of grasping a story, some children might actually find it boring or even difficult to read a printed book. But it can also work the other way: my 11-year-old son recently watched a TV adaptation of 'Sofie's World' by Jostein Gaarder that mostly covered the basic story, and not so much of the explanations of philosophy that the book provides. But then he started to read the 500-pages-long book, learning about Plato, Descartes and other philosophers. 
To summarize, I think it is important that we as a society are aware of the risks that comes with the glut of TV but also of the possibilities that are provided by it. We have to decide for our kids how much and what they are allowed to watch. If we approach this correctly, our kids will grow up to become well-rounded people with good knowledge, or else we run a risk of 'losing' a generation.
We should not forget that TV is entertainment, and that it should be allowed to be just that. There is nothing wrong with watching something just because it is fun.
